WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.499
Input validation: the boring checks that do the most work.

2
00:00:03.779 --> 00:00:06.147
Start with checks that need no model at all.

3
00:00:06.427 --> 00:00:11.973
They cost microseconds, they never hallucinate, and they close whole categories of problems.

4
00:00:12.253 --> 00:00:13.320
Size limits.

5
00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:18.528
Cap the characters or tokens a user can send, and the number of turns you will resend.

6
00:00:18.808 --> 00:00:32.824
The OWASP entry on unbounded consumption (LLM10) lists oversized and variable-length inputs as a way to exhaust compute and run up cost, and its first mitigation is validating and capping input size.

7
00:00:33.104 --> 00:00:37.541
Pair it with per-user rate limits and the per-request timeouts you built in week 1.

8
00:00:37.821 --> 00:00:39.016
Format and type.

9
00:00:39.296 --> 00:00:44.181
If a field should be a booking number, validate it as one before it ever goes near a prompt.

10
00:00:44.461 --> 00:00:48.003
If uploads should be PDFs under 10 MB, enforce that.

11
00:00:48.283 --> 00:00:52.763
Every input you can reject on type is one the model never has to interpret.

12
00:00:53.043 --> 00:00:54.429
PII redaction.

13
00:00:54.709 --> 00:01:04.288
If the task doesn’t need the user’s card number, phone, or government ID, remove it before it reaches the model, your logs, or a third-party classifier.

14
00:01:04.568 --> 00:01:10.392
Regular expressions plus checksums (the Luhn check for card numbers) catch the structured kinds.

15
00:01:10.672 --> 00:01:26.075
Libraries such as Microsoft’s Presidio, now a community project, combine named-entity recognition, patterns, and context, and its own documentation states that “there is no guarantee that Presidio will find all sensitive information.

16
00:01:26.355 --> 00:01:29.427
” Treat redaction as risk reduction, not a guarantee.

17
00:01:29.707 --> 00:01:34.507
Know the trade-off too: once you replace a phone number with [PHONE], the model can’t use it.

18
00:01:34.787 --> 00:01:39.587
Systems that need the value swap it for a reversible token kept server side.

19
00:01:39.867 --> 00:01:41.317
Injection heuristics.

20
00:01:41.597 --> 00:01:51.944
A handful of narrow patterns (“ignore previous instructions”, fake </system> tags, long base64 runs) block the laziest attacks at no cost.

21
00:01:52.224 --> 00:01:56.555
They are trivially bypassed by rephrasing or translation, so they are a floor.

22
00:01:56.835 --> 00:02:01.635
The lab measures exactly how much they catch and how many real customers they block.
